From 530d76824239ae59e8a7e9097530cf8be7edb834 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 22:44:25 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Show one view of a stereoscopic 3D video

A stereoscopic 3D video holds both views in every frame, in one of the
packings of the frame packing arrangement SEI (H.264 Table D-8, H.265
Table D.8) or of Matroska's StereoMode (RFC 9559), and was shown as it is
stored: both views squeezed or mixed into one picture. QtMultimedia now tells
the packing of the frames (QVideoFrameFormat::stereoMode), and a new
StereoFilter between the media player and the video output takes one view
out of every packing there is: side by side and top and bottom, side by side
with quincunx sampling, row and column interleaved, checkerboard and frame
sequential. The left view is shown at the shape of a view. Anaglyph videos
hold both views mixed and are shown as they are.

The application menu gets a 3D Video menu: the left view (the default), the
right view, or both views as the video holds them. For a video that says
nothing, or says it wrong, the Input Format list names every packing, with half
and full size views of side by side and top and bottom told apart, and Swap
Eyes turns the eyes round; the choice is for the file it was made for. The
stream does not say whether its views are half or full size, so the shape of
the frame decides: a side by side frame at least 2.5 times as wide as high, or
a top and bottom frame at most 1.2 times as wide as high, holds full size
views.

Needs QtMultimedia with QVideoFrameFormat::stereoMode (Qt 6.12).

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
